[2m› To the camp at Millholm[0m[39m[49m

[1m[38;2;236;230;217mthe camp at Millholm[0m[39m[49m
[38;2;110;106;98m────────────────────
[39mMillholm is half houses and half wagons with their wheels taken off, painted
saffron and rose and a deep blue. Cook-smoke drifts between them, smelling of
cumin and charred onion.
Your brother is sitting on a rail with your father’s old spear across his
knees, watching the road. He is taller by a head than when you went away.
The grey dog barks twice, for form’s sake, then comes to see who you are.

[2m› Talk to Osbert[0m[39m[49m
[3m‘There you are,’ says Osbert, as if you had stepped out an hour ago; only the[0m[39m[49m
[3mvoice gives it away.[0m[39m[49m

